# Committed fixtures are byte-exact, and a byte is a byte on every platform.
#
# Git's `core.autocrlf` rewrites line endings in text files on checkout, which
# on a Windows runner turns every committed `\n` into `\r\n` — and a test that
# compares a generated string against a committed file then fails on every line,
# reporting "drifted at line 1". That is a true statement about the bytes and a
# useless one about the program.
#
# So the fixtures are pinned. The tests normalise on read as well, because a
# checkout made before this file existed is still out there (TEST-016, #237).
crates/kmsrs-server/snapshots/*.txt   text eol=lf
docs/reference.md                     text eol=lf

# The wire vectors and the fuzz seeds are **binary**, not text-with-an-opinion.
# Several HTTP seeds carry `\r\n` *deliberately* — a request line without it is
# a different request, and a bare newline is one of the cases the parser is
# tested against (SEC-013, #306). Normalising them would silently rewrite the
# corpus into one that tests something else, and `fuzz_seeds.rs` asserts the
# committed bytes, so it would then fail on every checkout.
crates/kmsrs-vectors/vectors/*.bin    binary
fuzz/seeds/**                         binary

# Generated, and regenerated by a pipeline rather than edited. A diff against it
# is reviewed, not written (DB-002, #126).
crates/kmsrs-db/data/products.toml    text eol=lf linguist-generated=true
docs/reference.md                     linguist-generated=true
